                    Preamble

  The GNU Affero General Public License is a free, copyleft
license for free and open source software, required by the
GNU Affero General Public License (AGPL). It is designed
specifically for use in networked server applications.

  When you modify a program, your customers (whether they
receive the software over a network or on physical media)
should be able to download the source code corresponding to
the version they are running. If they cannot, then the
program is not truly free software.

  This license is designed for programs that run on servers
and serve users over a network. It ensures that users who
interact with the program over a network can access the
source code.

                    Core Terms

  0. Definitions.

  "This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU Affero
General Public License.

  "Copyright" means copyright under the laws of a country.

  "The Program" refers to any copyrightable work covered
by this License.

  "Source code" means the preferred form of a work for
making modifications to it.

  "Modified version" means any work containing the Program
or a portion of it, either verbatim or with modifications.

  "Covered work" means an unmodified version of the Program
or a modified version of the Program.

  "Convey" means any operation that enables a third party
to make or receive a copy of a covered work.

  "Interact with users through a computer network" means
communicating with users through a computer network, with
no transfer of a copy of the work.

  "Remote network interaction" means interacting with users
through a computer network, with no transfer of a copy of
the work.

  1. Source Code.

  The "source code" for a covered work means the complete
object code for the work, with all related interface
definitions, plus the scripts used to control compilation
and installation of the object code.

  2. Basic Permissions.

  All rights not expressly granted under this License are
reserved.

  3. AGPL Requirements.

  If you modify the Program, your modified version must
prominently offer all users interacting with it remotely
through a computer network an opportunity to receive the
source code for the modified version.

